<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>&laquo;I do not know how to pray&raquo;</h4>
<p>It is almost certain that some participants will say this. Normalize the experience: we all start from zero. Prayer is not a technique to master, but a relationship to cultivate. Saint Teresa said prayer is &laquo;close friendship.&raquo; No one is born knowing how to be a friend; we learn over time.</p>

<h4>Prayer is not only asking</h4>
<p>Many people identify prayer with &laquo;asking for things.&raquo; It is important to show the other dimensions: adoration, thanksgiving, praise, silence, listening. Saint Augustine said prayer is &laquo;the affection of the heart directed toward God.&raquo; It includes everything: joy, sadness, confusion, gratitude.</p>

<h4>God thirsts for us</h4>
<p>The image of the Samaritan woman is very powerful. Jesus asks for a drink, but he has come to give living water. This is how God is: he seems to need something from us, but he is the one coming to give us everything. If the group grasps this idea, the class is won. Prayer is not a duty to fulfill, but a response to the love that precedes us.</p>

<h4>Distractions in prayer</h4>
<p>Someone might ask: &laquo;What do I do if I get distracted?&raquo; The Catechism speaks of distractions as a normal experience: &laquo;The struggle against distractions is the normal struggle of prayer&raquo; (CCC 2729). There is no need to be anxious. Simply, when we notice, gently return to God's presence.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use the image of Jesus talking with his Father God. Teach them that praying is like talking to a friend who always listens. Activity: draw Jesus praying on the mountain or write a letter to God. Memorize: &laquo;Pray without ceasing&raquo; with hand gestures.</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect with their experience of digital communication. &laquo;Prayer is like liking God's life&raquo; &mdash; but a sincere like. Talk about the difference between praying out of habit and truly praying. Invite a minute of silence at the beginning of class to experience contemplative prayer.</p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults have a mechanical idea of prayer: praying is &laquo;saying prayers.&raquo; Help them discover that prayer is a living relationship. Your own testimony of how prayer changed your life is very powerful. Invite them to start with 5 minutes daily, using a psalm or the Our Father.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the three stages of prayer according to Saint Teresa (vocal prayer, meditation, contemplation). &laquo;The Way of Perfection&raquo; is an excellent resource. Speak about the prayer of quiet and transforming union. Cite Saint John of the Cross on the dark night as purification of prayer.</p>

</section>

