<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>&laquo;If God already knows what I need, why pray?&raquo;</h4>
<p>This is one of the most common questions. The answer is that prayer is not to inform God of our needs, but for us to become aware that we depend on him. It does not change God; it changes us. As the Catechism says, prayer purifies our desire and aligns us with God's will (CCC 2567).</p>

<h4>&laquo;God does not listen to me; I prayed and nothing happened&raquo;</h4>
<p>God always listens, but he does not always answer as we expect. Jesus prayed in Gethsemane asking that the cup pass from him, and the cup did not pass. But he received the strength to drink it. Sometimes what we ask is not what is best for us. &laquo;Your Father in heaven gives good things to those who ask him&raquo; (Mt 7:11). &laquo;Good things&raquo; does not always mean &laquo;what we ask&raquo;; it means what we truly need.</p>

<h4>&laquo;Why pray with words if God knows our hearts?&raquo;</h4>
<p>Words help us concretize, give shape to what we feel. Jesus himself prayed with words. Vocal prayer is not for God; it is for us: it helps us focus, commit, and unite body and soul in prayer. Moreover, community prayer requires words to pray together (CCC 2702).</p>

<h4>&laquo;What if I feel nothing when I pray?&raquo;</h4>
<p>This is normal. Prayer does not depend on feelings. Saint Teresa spent years of &laquo;dryness&raquo; in prayer, and those were the years of greatest spiritual growth. What matters is not feeling, but being present with fidelity. &laquo;Prayer does not consist in thinking a lot, but in loving a lot&raquo; (Saint Teresa).</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord, you who prayed to the Father before all things,<br />
teach me to pray so that I may teach others.<br />
May my prayer not be routine, but encounter.<br />
May my words not be empty, but sincere.<br />
May my silence not be absence, but presence.<br />
And when others see my life of prayer,<br />
may they too want to know you.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

